<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arta, Greece</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Arta is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>, north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arachthos">Arachthos</a> river near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambracian-gulf">Ambracian Gulf</a>, standing on the site of ancient Ambracia. In the 13th century it was the capital of the Despotate of Epirus, whose rulers built Byzantine churches such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panagia-parigoritissa">Panagia Parigoritissa</a>. Its old stone bridge over the Arachthos is famous from Greek folk song.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arta-greece.jpg" alt="Arta, Greece" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Apaleutos25 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%CE%86%CF%81%CF%84%CE%B1,_Arta.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first settlement in the area dates to the 9th century BCE. Ambracia was founded as a colony of Corinth in the 7th century BCE. In 294 BCE it was given to Pyrrhus, king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molossians">Molossians</a> and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>, who made it his capital and a base for attacking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, and adorned it with a palace, temples and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatres</a>. In 146 BCE it became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a>. Arta is first attested under that name in 1082, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> under Bohemond besieged it. In the Komnenian period it flourished as a commercial centre with links to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, and by 1157 it was a bishopric.</p><p>After the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a>, the Partitio Romaniae of 1204 assigned the city to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, but in 1205 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-i-komnenos-doukas">Michael I Komnenos Doukas</a> came to Arta and founded the principality known as the Despotate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>. The city remained its capital for most of its history, and it saw much building: the Church of the Parigoritissa and the Church of the Kato Panagia, and fortifications after 1227. Its exports to Venice and Ragusa included dried meat, lard, ham, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigo">indigo</a>.</p><p>Later the city changed hands often. Troops of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-nicaea">Empire of Nicaea</a> took it after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pelagonia">Battle of Pelagonia</a> in 1259, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-doukas">John I Doukas</a> soon recovered it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andronikos-ii-palaiologos">Andronikos II Palaiologos</a> attacked it unsuccessfully in 1292, and the Angevins besieged it in 1303. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> passed to the Orsini family in 1318 and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andronikos-iii-palaiologos">Andronikos III Palaiologos</a> in 1335. Arta fell to the Serbian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-dusan">Stefan Dušan</a> in 1347, was held by Albanian rulers from about 1367, and was captured by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlo-i-tocco">Carlo I Tocco</a> in 1416. In 1449 it fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, who called it Narda.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Arta lies on the Arachthos River, on the site of ancient Ambracia, whose remains include walls, the ruins of a temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> and a small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>. The Castle of Arta was built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-i-komnenos-doukas">Michael I Komnenos Doukas</a> in the early 13th century, though its present form is largely post-Byzantine. The palace of the Despots has vanished completely, but many churches survive, among them the cathedral Church of the Paregoretissa, built about 1290 by Nikephoros I Komnenos Doukas and his wife Anna Palaiologina Kantakouzene, and the Monastery of Saint Theodora, which holds the tomb of the city's patron, Theodora of Arta. The city was annexed to the Greek Kingdom in 1881.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Arta, Greece” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
